confirmed.fix:
In place_pages (model.hpp:2963-2967) let the cursor catch up to the page centre instead of stepping once:

  while (worker_cumsum[worker] < page_center && worker + 1 < worker_cumsum.size()) {
    ++worker;
    work_cumsum += prior_work(worker);
  }

Verified by simulation: with the loop, head k's pending pages land inside head k's ready block in both cases (1 user / 8 heads / 32 workers: workers 1, 5, 9, ..., 29; 8 users / 4 heads / 40 workers: eight distinct workers of each 10-worker block). If the kill-switch path (TRON_PLAN_WEIGHTED_DISABLE=1, place_pages({0, 1})) must stay byte-for-byte the old walk, pass a flag into place_pages and keep the single `if` for that call; otherwise the loop is also harmless there (it differs only when one page's centre skips a whole worker interval). Then rewrite the comment at model.hpp:2894-2897: the pending pages of a KV head land in the same worker block as its ready pages because the cursor catches up to the head's share on every page, not because the two passes have equal shares (equal shares alone do not place pages when a pass has fewer pages per head than workers per head). Add a unit test in t/t_llama_unit.cpp with few pending pages per head (e.g. 1 pending page, 8 KV heads, 32 workers) asserting that every worker holding a pending section of head k also holds a ready section of head k.

At the top of the test body (after the TEST_CASE line at t/t_llama_unit.cpp:3471-3472), add a named precondition instead of the comment-only note:

  INFO("this test covers the weighted plan: unset TRON_PLAN_WEIGHTED_DISABLE");
  REQUIRE(plan_weighted_enabled());

plan_weighted_enabled is in namespace tron and the file has `using namespace tron;` [t/t_llama_unit.cpp:84], so no include or qualification change is needed. Keep it a REQUIRE, not a WARN-and-return: a skip would hide the only unit coverage of the weighted plan when the switch is set, which the Wade checklist flags; a failure that names the switch is the right form. Then shorten the comment at 3469-3470 to point at the REQUIRE (for example "The REQUIRE below names the precondition: the switch is read once per process, so the test cannot set it").
